hi there steve kaufman here i want to  talk about vocabulary primarily  and vocabulary learning using this  term interleaving which was a term that  i introduced  in my previous video which is based on  recent research into how we learn and  which says that  rather than deliberate focused  learning of something  we're better off to  learn two or three things and go back to  them and  basically meander around and stagger and  intersperse or interleave our learning  that  those people who study very deliberately  in blocks in massive blocks may do  better in the short term but in the long  term they retain less  than people who  do different tasks or work on different  skills  forget come back do it again and so  forth  apparently one of the  uh and i suggest that you can google the  term interleave l-e-a-v-e interleaved  and find a fair amount of uh  articles on it and some people in the  comments to my previous video said you  know this is just a new term for  something that's been known  a long time  but i wanted to talk a little bit about  vocabulary  and and talk about some of the comments  that the previous video  seemed to elicit one person said you  know bruce lee said that i don't fear  the person who has practiced ten  thousand kicks once i hear the person  who has practiced one kick ten thousand  times  yeah if your objective was to learn one  kick  then that's a good strategy  however in language learning we don't  need to learn one kick or one word we  need to learn  an awful lot of words we need to learn  tens of thousands of words eventually if  we want to achieve fluency we need to  learn to recognize them eventually to  get a sense of how they're used together  with other words eventually we want to  learn to use them ourselves  it's a gradual  process  and it covers lots of words and phrases  that are you know made up of these words  so it's not a matter of learning one  kick so i think that's not a relevant uh  comparison at all  another person  perhaps on that video or on another  video was commenting on the number of  words that i seem to have learned  during my 90 day korean challenge and if  i look at my statistics during those 90  days i added 10 000 words to my known  words total  and i saved  9820 links that means to say i saved  9820 words and phrases  converted them from blue into yellow  um  i read 78 000 words during that period  and i listened to all of that at least  once usually many many times  and i spoke for 13 hours so those are  the statistics now  10 000 new known words  one thing about words the way we count  them at link it's very important to  realize that  every change every inflection in the  word is counted as a separate word i  prefer that because  if i learn  the word a particular word in this tense  or in this case if it's a noun and i see  it again  a i might have forgotten that i ever  looked it up  so or you know the previous form of it  so the idea of saving that word again in  a different form  strengthens my ability to  identify the meaning the scope of that  word and as i see the word in different  forms and i save them  that helps me get a sense of the  overall way that word is used so uh  obviously you can't compare that to a  number based on word families but that's  how we count them so 10 000 words and  close to 10 000 words saved now of the  9820 words that i say words and phrases  of course i don't know them all if i go  through the list in my vocabulary  section that link  i'll find that the most frequent words  these are four star words at link i'll  know most of them 70 or 80  because they've appeared frequently  enough i've seen them in different  contexts i know them even without  studying them in flashcards i know them  the three star words i know well it's  still pretty close those  two most frequent groups i'm going to be  in the 70 that was in my experience  once you get down to the two star words  and the one star words it falls 50 40  30. so i really don't 