bulary and really focus on  the  basics of grammar  i personally think that's very hard to  do very few people can do that i can  focus on the grammar as i did in the  past for say german cases  try to learn it try to memorize it  repeat it to myself it doesn't stick  however if i learn lots of words through  this interleaving process  say phrases see the language in action  occasionally review the grammar rules  again interleaving it go back to reading  and listening gradually it starts to  stick so in both cases and i think to  some extent  grammar learning is related to  vocabulary learning to the extent that  every word in its different  form and the different endings that it  has  uh  represents  a form of  the grammar  put together in phrases they represent  the patterns of the language and the  more we are exposed to these patterns in  different uh contexts  the more we we become used to them and  that ultimately that's a better way  of getting a sense of the grammar  than deliberately studying the grammar  and we can go a long way  in listening and reading without having  the grammar nailed down  i mean the ukrainian i'm doing now is  not a good example because the grammar  is so similar to russian  but even when i did  you know romanian or other languages  occasionally refer to the grammar which  is quite different from say italian but  i don't need to have it all nailed down  i don't need to know  the cases or or any number of different  things the funny futures that they have  or in iranian in order to  be able to understand and as long as i  can understand i can continue  interleaving continue exposing myself to  the language  uh so  to summarize again i think  for vocabulary acquisition and by  implication for grammar acquisition  uh interleaving just absorbing it  uh  gradually letting it settle in there  different settlements  is the way to go in my opinion and i'm  sure i'll have people with different  perspectives on this and i look forward  to your